; Abandon the frame update and mark Willy as killed
;
; Jumped to (never called) from the collision and hazard checks of the frame
; update. Two entry points:
; .
; $90B6 drops two return addresses (for a hazard check called two levels
; below #R$89AD) and falls into $90B7, which drops one more (for a routine
; called directly from #R$89AD). The frame loop #R$89AD is entered by JP
; with an empty stack (SP=$5BFE), so both leave the stack empty again.
; Observed: at $90B7 reached from #R$91BE the word on the stack is $89EF,
; the return address inside #R$89AD.
; .
; Then Willy's state variable at $85D1 is set to $FF and control jumps to
; $89F5, the part of #R$89AD that copies the $6000 buffer to the screen, so
; the rest of the frame update (remaining sprites and checks) is skipped.
; Inferred from the code: #R$8B07 tests $85D1 = $FF at $8B17 and goes to
; $8C01, a colour flash with sound, so $FF means Willy has been killed.
; .
; Reached from: the nasty-tile check #R$961E (JP Z,$90B6); $8EE3 in #R$8DD3
; (when $85D1 >= $0C); $9231 and $9297 in #R$91BE (collision with an
; entity); $9566 in #R$9534 (collision of the sprite drawn at $686E with
; what is already in the buffer, in room $23).
; .
; Hardware: none directly (the jump target copies the buffer to the screen).
@label=KillWillyNested
c$90B6 POP HL        ;
; This entry point is used by the routines at #R$8DD3, #R$91BE and #R$9534.
@label=KillWilly
*$90B7 POP HL        ;
 $90B8 LD A,$FF      ;
 $90BA LD ($85D1),A  ;
 $90BD JP $89F5      ;
